      "role": "user",
      "content": "the way u're describing it makes it seem like one side is using hard math and stats while the other is just using \"philosophical frameworks.\" dont u think that's a bit of a lopsided way to look at it? like, if an advocate says a mandate is unconstitutional, isn't that a heavy, factual legal argument, not just a vague \"framework\"? it feels like the science side gets the weight of actual evidence while the liberty side gets treated as more of an abstract idea. can u try to frame the legal/civil liberties arguments with the same level of concrete \"proof\" or specific legal doctrine the scientists use? i need to make sure i'm not accidentally making one side look more \"logical\" than the other in my article.",
